  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 149 PM CDT Sat Oct 24 2026\n\nMonday/Tuesday...\n\nAforementioned wave will eject off of the Rockies and track \nnortheast towards the northern CONUS. As this tracks northeast, the \nridge overhead will continue to flatten and get shunted east of the \narea, with upper level southwesterly flow setting up over the \nregion. LLVL southwesterly flow will also increase, with a surface \nwarm front slowly passing through the area.  As such, we will \ncontinue to see above normal temperatures in the low-mid 70s. As was \nmentioned in the previous forecast package, we will have a weak\nboundary nearby and subtle upper level forcing moving in. This \nwas favoring low-end chances for precipitation, but confidence \nwas low due to limited moisture. Latest forecast runs have come \nin largely dry through this timeframe, owing to too much dry \nair. Thus, will message a dry forecast, with passing clouds \nbeing the main sign of the slight increase in moisture. \n\nWednesday Onward...\n\nBroad upper level southwest flow will set up over the central CONUS, \nwith persistent llvl warm advection into the region. Thus, \ntemperatures will remain above seasonal normals, with most in the \nmid 70s through the end of the week. Later in the week and into the \nweekend, we see stronger waves work into the region, ejecting off of \nthe Rockies. These waves will increase southwesterly flow into the \narea, with better moisture working in. Thus, we will start to see \nprecipitation chances increase midweek and beyond. Chances will \nincrease for areas along and north of a line from Fairfield to \nDubuque Iowa, expanding to the remainder of the area as we go into \nthe upcoming weekend (30-60% chances Saturday into Sunday).",
